# 1. Introduction

1. IntroductionConverting solar energy into electric energy is an aussichtsreich technique that can meet the growing requirement for sustainable energy. Especially, polymer solar cells have acquired widespread research interest in the scientific community on account of the capability as cost-effective flexible power generators [1–3]. In the past few years, the improvement in PCE by engineering the interface charge extraction characteristics [4], optimizing the nanoscale morphology of the bulk-heterojunction (BHJ) layers [5,6], and designing the novel photoactive materials [7,8], has been the main research focus in the field of organic photovoltaics. These booming advances make the PSC devices gradually develop in a more practical direction. In particular, it is worth mentioning that developing an appropriate charge transport layer (CTL) is one of the excellent approaches to ameliorate the efficiency of cells. A suitable CTL contributes to forming a barrier-free contact between the electrode and the BHJ layer, which will reduce the energy loss during the charge extraction. The conventional PSC structures are usually based on a conductive ITO anode and the most common PEDOT: PSS hole transport layer [9]. In order to avoid the employment of easily oxidized low work function (WF) metal electrodes and hygroscopic PEDOT:PSS CTL, an inverted device configuration, where the ITO/FTO side of the PSC is used as a cathode terminal, has been widely utilized to achieve prolonged long-term stability and be compatible with roll-to-roll fabrication technology in the field of PSCs.In the inverted PSCs, solution-processed metal oxides, especially zinc oxide (ZnO), are widely used ETL materials by virtue of the superior electron transport characteristics, outstanding environmental stability, and easy solution processability to form a smooth and well-defined layer [10–12]. Nevertheless, the photovoltaic performances of the devices based on these metal oxides are not always desirable [13]. In consequence, additional modification on the ETL is generally required to further optimize the interface characteristic, enhance the electron transfer from the acceptor and boost the built-in potential [14–16]. Upon adopting an alkali-metal carbonate [17], polar solvent [18], polyelectrolyte [19], or self-assembled monolayer [20] to construct electric dipole layers, the WFs have been effectively regulated to enhance the electron transport by altering the vacuum energy level \((E_{\nu a c})\) at the interfaces. Typically, Zhang et al. presented an efficient PTB7: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) - based inverted PSC using the ZnO ETL and carbon quantum dots (C-QDs) bilayer interfacial layer to achieve a maximum efficiency of \(9.64\%\) [21]. In comparison with the bare ZnO-based cell, the employment of ZnO/C-QD bilayer possesses superiorities of reducing the charge recombination loss, enhancing the charge collection probability as well as inhibiting the exciton quenching in polymer solar cells. Although these strategies present positive influences on the device efficiency of the PSCs, their practicability has also been limited to some extent on account of the difficulty in obtaining the suggested materials and the narrow (confined) selection windows for organic salts [22,23].In terms of the electron collection contact, one of the crucial factors is to block the injection of holes in the active layer, so as to suppress the unfavorable recombination at the interface. In general, the carrier recombination also exists at the interface of the FTO electrodes and ZnO charge transport layers. One of the effective strategies to control the charge recombination at this interface is to incorporate a wide band-gap semiconductor on the surface of FTO. Typically, MgO is well-known to be an insulator with a wide energy bandgap and good tunneling characteristic [24]. Recently, MgO with an excellent hole-blocking feature has been reported to successfully suppress the electron/hol