In the inverted PSCs, solution-processed metal oxides, especially zinc oxide (ZnO), are widely used ETL materials by virtue of the superior electron transport characteristics, outstanding environmental stability, and easy solution processability to form a smooth and well-defined layer [10–12]. Nevertheless, the photovoltaic performances of the devices based on these metal oxides are not always desirable [13]. In consequence, additional modification on the ETL is generally required to further optimize  the interface characteristic, enhance the electron transfer from the acceptor and boost the built-in potential [14–16]. Upon adopting an alkali­ metal carbonate [17], polar solvent [18], polyelectrolyte [19], or self-assembled monolayer [20] to construct electric dipole layers, the WFs have been effectively regulated to enhance the electron transport by altering the vacuum energy level \((E_{\nu a c})\) at the interfaces. Typically, Zhang et al. presented an efficient PTB7: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) -based inverted PSC using the ZnO ETL and carbon quantum dots (C-QDs) bilayer interfacial layer to achieve a maximum efficiency of \(9.64\%\) [21]. In comparison with the bare ZnO-based cell, the employment of ZnO/C-QD bilayer possesses superiorities of reducing the charge recombination loss, enhancing the charge collection probability as well as inhibiting the exciton quenching in polymer solar cells. Although these  strategies present positive influences on the device efficiency of the PSCs, their practicability has also been limited to some extent on account of the difficulty in obtaining the suggested materials and the narrow (confined) selection windows for organic salts [22,23].

In terms of the electron collection contact, one of the crucial factors is to block the injection of holes in the active layer, so as to suppress the unfavorable recombination at the interface. In general, the carrier recombination also exists at the interface of the FTO electrodes and ZnO charge transport layers. One of the effective strategies to control the charge recombination at this interface is to incorporate a wide band-gap semiconductor on the surface of FTO. Typically, MgO is well-known to  be an insulator with a wide energy bandgap and good tunneling characteristic [24]. Recently, MgO with an excellent hole-blocking feature has been reported to successfully suppress the electron/hole recombination loss within a titania compact layer and improve the energy conversion efficiency in

perovskite solar cells [25]. Han and co-workers modified the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer with an ultrathin MgO nanolayer via a sol-gel process. The \(\mathrm{MgO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) composite films can inhibit the photo-generated carrier recombination at the surface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\). Thus, both \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and FF of the device are significantly reinforced, engendering an enhanced PCE of \(12.7\%\) [26]. In addition, it is reported in the literature that an ultra-thin MgO film is inserted between the \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) interfacial layer and bottom electrode in planar perovskite solar cells [27]. Compared with the control device, all \(J_{-}V\) parameters of the MgO-based device showed a notable elevation, resulting in an efficiency as high as \(18.23\%\), with an increment of about \(11\%\). It is worth mentioning that the thickness of the MgO film has a greater impact on the efficiency of the device. A too thick MgO interlayer may exhibit insulating property because of the lower conductivity, thus increasing the series resistance of PSCs. Up to now, the feasibility of using MgO interlayer in constructing high-efficiency polymer solar cells is yet unclear and has not been reported.

Therefore, we propose an alternative approach to further improve the device performance by integrating the superiorities of the MgO film and ZnO nanoparticles. In other words, the electron extracting capabilities of ZnO are merged with the hole blocking characteristics of MgO to improve the efficiency of electron harvesting at the cathode. In this contribution, a \(\mathrm{MgO}/\mathrm{ZnO}\) stacked structure was incorporated into the PTB7-Th: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) -based inverted PSCs to combine the superiorities of both ZnO ETL and MgO layer. As expected, the PSCs based on the \(\mathrm{MgO}/\mathrm{ZnO}\) composite films exhibit overwhelming photovoltaic performance of \(11.02\%\), with tremendous increments in all the device parameters in comparison to those of the control devices (PCE \(8.79\%\)). In the study, one of the problems to be solved is whether a thinner MgO layer may ameliorate the contact of ZnO without causing injurious charge recombination pathways. Through the analysis of a series of characterization measurement results, such as AFM, UPS, PL, EIS, the working mechanism of improving the device efficiency was obtained. The improved photovoltaic performance of PSCs with the \(\mathrm{MgO}/\mathrm{ZnO}\) stacked films may be ascribed to the reduced roughness and interface defects of FTO substrates after inserting the MgO interlayer, which provides a splendid growth platform for the formation of homogeneous and high-quality ZnO ETL. Furthermore, we show the MgO films to promote the electron transport and block the hole injection effectively, leading to a suppressed photogenerated carrier recombination and an elevated electron collection at the interface. We believe that our research work will lay a good foundation for the development of high-performance PSCs.

# 2. Experimental

# 2.1. Materials

2. Experimental2.1. MaterialsThe FTO glasses were provided by the Dongguan Xiangcheng Group. PTB7-Th and \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) were ordered from 1-Material Chem­ scitech Inc. 1,8-Diiodooctane (DIO) was supplied by TCI Chemicals. Isopropanol \((99.7\%)\) , acetone \((99.5\%)\) , toluene \((99.5\%)\) , and ethanol \((99.5\%)\) were received from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Chlo­ roform \((99.5\%)\) ,  chlorobenzene \((99.8\%)\) , \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{CH}3\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0\) \((99.0\%)\) , methanol \((99.8\%)\) , \(\mathtt{M o O}_{3}\) \((99.9\%)\) , and \(n\) -butanol \((99.8\%)\) were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. Zinc acetatedehydrate \((99\%)\) was provided by Alfa Aesar. All the materials can be used directly without further purification.2.2. Metal oxide nanoparticles synthesisThe zinc acetate dehydrate \((1.18\mathrm{~g})\) was dissolved in \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) of anhydrous methanol and stirred at \(65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) . Afterwards,

# 2.2. Metal oxide nanoparticles synthesis

The zinc acetate dehydrate \((1.18\mathrm{~g})\) was dissolved in \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) of anhydrous methanol and stirred at \(65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) . Afterwards,
25 mL of a KOH methanol solution was slowly added to the above solution within 10 min and stirred at \(0.5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{~h~}\). The ZnO nanoparticles were separated from the reaction solution and washed three times with methanol. Subsequently, the ZnO precipitate was added to a mixed solution of \(n\)-butanol, methanol and chloroform to obtain the ZnO solution. For the synthesis of \(\tt M g O\) precursor solution, the magnesium acetate tetrahydrate was added to the ethanol and stirred at room temperature overnight.

# 2.3. Solar cell fabrication

The FTO substrates were rinsed by toluene, ethanol, DI water, and isopropyl alcohol. The cleaned glasses were baked for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) and then treated with \(0_{2}\) plasma. After that, the \(\tt M g O\) precursor solutions of various concentrations (0.01 M, \(0.03\mathrm{~M~}\) and \(0.05\mathrm{~M~}\) were spin-coated on the FTO at the rotation speeds of \(5000~\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30~s\) to optimize the film thickness and then heated at \(200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(60\;\mathrm{min}\). Then the substrates were subjected to ultraviolet irradiation for \(15~\mathrm{min}\). The prepared ZnO precursor was deposited on the \(\tt M g O\) films at \(4500~\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(40~s\), and annealed at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) to remove the unnecessary solvent. The PTB7-Th and \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\)-BM with a weight ratio of 1:1.5 were added to the chlorobenzene solvent and \(3\ \mathrm{vol\%}\) of DIO additive and then stirred at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight. Then the BHJ layer was spin coated at \(1000~\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(60~s\) in a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) glovebox. Finally, \(5\ \mathrm{nm}\ \mathrm{MoO}_{3}\) and \(100\ \mathrm{nm\ Ag}\) anode were deposited on the BHJ layer.

# 2.4. Device characterizations

The AFM images were tested using asylum research cypher microscopes. The photocurrent density-voltage \((I{-}V)\) curves were detected with a Keithley 2400 source meter. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) images were characterized with a Bruker D8 Advance

The AFM images were tested using asylum research cypher microscopes. The photocurrent density-voltage \((I{-}V)\) curves were detected with a Keithley 2400 source meter. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) images were characterized with a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer. The incident photon to current efficiency (IPCE) was obtained by QEX10 measurement system. The steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra were recorded on FLS 900 fluorescence spectrometer. The scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were monitored by Hitachi S900. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were assessed by using Philips CM200 instrument at \(200~\mathrm{kV}\).

# 3. Results and discussion

The crystalline characteristic of the \(\tt M g O\) layer is ascertained using the XRD measurement in Fig. 1a. The appearance of XRD characteristic peaks suggests the formation and good crystallinity of \(\tt M g O\) nanoparticles. The characteristic diffraction peaks, including (111), (200), (220), (311), and (222), are indexed into a face-centered cubic phase structure, which matches well with a standard JCPDF card (No. 87-0653). In addition, it can be observed that no impurity phases were detected. XPS measurement was applied to manifest the existence of \(\tt M g O\) nanolayers. The peak at \(49.16~\mathrm{eV}\) is assigned to the \(\tt M g\ 2p\), while the binding energy of \(531.45~\mathrm{eV}\) corresponds to the characteristic of O 1s (Fig. 1b and c). The TEM image in Fig. 1d shows that the sample consists of unevenly distributed nanoparticles. The prepared \(\tt M g O\) nanoparticles are extremely small and the diameters are approximately \(5-\) \(10\,\textrm{n m}\). Fig. 1d presents the selective area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern. Evidently, a battery of concentric diffraction rings can be detected, indicative of the polycrystalline structure of the synthesized \(\tt M g O\) samples.

The absorption spectra of the active layer with various interfacial layers are compared in Fig. 2a. It is clear that both samples present eminent absorbance in the visible range, which illustrates that the presence of \(\tt M g O\) film hardly influenced the light absorption efficiency of the BHJ layer because of the superior light trans

