The surface morphology and roughness of the samples are investigated by the tapping-mode AFM measurement (Fig. 3). The bare FTO substrate possesses a relatively rough surface with a root mean square (RMS) roughness of \(30.4\ \mathrm{nm}\), and there are some gaps between the FTO crystals. After the \(\tt M g O\) film was spin-coated, the gaps between the FTO crystals are partially filled and the film presents a relatively uniform and smooth surface morphology, leading to a reduced RMS roughness of \(19.6~\mathrm{nm}\). Therefore, the surface defects of the FTO substrate are passivated, which is favorable of the suppressed carriers recombination in the devices [27]. In addition, the uniform distribution of FTO film surface will provide a splendid growth platform for the formation of homogeneous and high-quality ZnO ETL.

To obtain the energy level of \(\tt M g O\) layer, the UPS measurement was conducted. The valence band maximum (VBM) can be extracted by the following expression [28]

\[
VBM=h\nu-(E_{cutoff}-E_{onset})
\]

where \(h\nu\) represents the emission energy. It appears from Fig. 4a and b that the binding energies at the cutoff and valence region

are \(17.43\ \mathrm{eV}\) and \(4.88\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively. Therefore, the VBM value of \(\tt M g O\) is calculated to be \(8.67\ \mathrm{eV}\). Since the reported higher bandgap of \(\tt M g O\) (\(\sim\!7.5\;\mathrm{eV}\)) [29,30], the minimum conduction band (CBM) is estimated to be \(1.17\ \mathrm{eV}\). To probe the application potential of the \(\tt M g O\) interlayer in cells, the inverted PSCs were manufactured by using the high performance polymer-fullerene blend PTB7-Th and \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) as the BHJ layer (Fig. 4c). The corresponding energy level diagram is displayed in Fig. 4d. Encouragingly, the ZnO film serves as an efficient ETL, while the \(\tt M g O\) layer can block the injection of holes effectively and thus suppress the recombination of photoexcited charges due to the wider bandgap and the lower valence band position. Meanwhile, since the \(\tt M g O\) film is quite thin and possesses a wonderful tunneling characteristic, the photogenerated electrons may readily tunnel through the \(\tt M g O\) layer [27].

Fig. 5a exhibits the \(J_{\mathrm{c}}{-}V\) characteristics of the cells with an architecture of \(\mathrm{FTO/MgO/ZnO/PTB7-Th:PC_{71}BM/MoO_{3}/Ag}\) employing various concentrations of \(\tt M g O\) precursor solution. The photovoltaic parameters of the fabricated devices are presented in Table 1. It is clear that the PSC with ZnO-only interlayer shows a typical performance with a short-circuit current density \((J_{s c})\) of \(17.00\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\), fill factor (FF) of \(66.32\%\), open-circuit voltage \((V_{o c})\) of 0.78 and PCE of \(8.79\%\), respectively. By introducing a \(\tt M g O\) interlayer with a precursor concentration of \(0.01\textbf{M}\) on the ZnO ETL, all the \(J_{\mathrm{c}}{-}V\) parameters are obviously elevated \((J_{s c}=18.75\) \(\mathrm{mA\cm}^{-2}\), \(F F=69.61\%\), and \(V_{o c}=0.79\ \mathrm{V}\)), leading to an apparently enhanced performance of \(10.30\%\). Very encouragingly, the \(\tt M g O\)-based devices with a concentration of \(0.03\mathrm{~M~}\) obtained an optimal PCE of \(11.02\%\) with an enhanced \(J_{s c}\) of \(19.28\mathrm{\mA\,cm}^{-2}\), FF of \(71.51\%\), and \(V_{o c}\) of \(0.80\,\mathrm{~V~}\). However, the device performance degraded

