To gain a deeper understanding of the inherent mechanism of the photovoltaic performance improvement, we plotted the probable electron transfer process in the devices, as illustrated in Fig. 6. In terms of the pure ZnO-containing cells (Fig. 6a), the surface defects and trap states are formed from the physical contact between FTO and ZnO ETL due to the rough surface of the FTO film, which inevitably restricts the photoexcited electrons and causes the electrons recombination at the interface. As a contrast, as presented in Fig. 6b, the thickness of the interfacial layer is increased and some of the interfacial defects are filled upon inserting the MgO interlayer, which can block the holes and reduce the carrier recombination more effectively, thus enhancing the photovoltaic performance of the PSCs.

The photo-induced charge transfer behavior of solar cells is examined by steady-state PL spectra [38,39]. Higher PL quenching efficiency demonstrates more efficient charge transfer and less charge recombination loss in the photoactive layer, which is beneficial for achieving higher photocurrent in the devices [40–42]. Fig. 7 presents the steady-state PL spectra of the PTB7-Th donor without and with MgO film prepared under optimal device conditions. It is clear that the PTB7-Th film with MgO presents a greater PL quenching efficiency than that of the reference film, which

