The dark \( J_{-}V \) characteristics of inverted cells are exhibited in Fig. 9a. The dark current in the \( \mathrm{MgO} \)-based PSCs under reverse bias is about one order of magnitude lower than in the control cell. The lower dark current density implies that the current leakage and the biomolecular recombination can be suppressed in the \( \mathrm{MgO} \) embedded device [44]. Thus, the optimized device based on the \( \mathrm{MgO} \) interlayer is expected to present a higher photocurrent.

Furthermore, the lower dark current density also shows that \( \mathrm{MgO} \) helps to block the holes at the cathode, which finally increases the efficiency of the devices to \( 11.02\% \) [45]. The interfacial dynamics of the studied PSCs was elucidated by the EIS measurement [46]. The Nyquist plots with the \( \mathrm{MgO} \) and \( \mathrm{MgO/ZnO} \) interlayers at zero bias are presented in Fig. 9b. According to the equivalent circuit model, the series resistance \( \left(R_{1}\right) \) is correlated with the sheet resistance of the electrodes and the electrical contacts. The charge transport resistance \( \left(R_{2}\right) \) is associated with the resistance at the electrode/BHJ layer interface and inside the active layer [47,48]. The decreased transport resistance reveals that the \( \mathrm{MgO} \)-based PSC exhibits a lower contact resistance, contributing to the increased charge extraction probability. As discussed above, these results corroborate that the insertion of \( \mathrm{MgO} \) interfacial layer is beneficial to the efficient operation of inverted photovoltaic devices.

